The goal is a real server-rendered public documentation experience.

Requirements:

1. Initial HTML must contain the main public content, rather than requiring a browser-side API request before meaningful content appears.
2. Keep data fetching in Server Components.
3. Keep Client Components limited to actual interactions.
4. Avoid unnecessary hydration.
5. Avoid repeated sequential API calls when requests can safely run in parallel.
6. Avoid unbounded API requests.
7. Use existing pagination and summary DTOs.
8. Do not fetch every lesson body when rendering a course outline.
9. Avoid excessive client-side state for data already owned by the server.
10. Preserve responsive rendering and accessibility.

Do not claim that implementing SSR automatically guarantees a particular PageSpeed or Core Web Vitals score.

Measure performance only if the project has an existing reliable measurement workflow. Report actual measured values separately from targets.

